  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 933 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe elongated upper- level ridge over Tennessee/Kentucky and mid-\nlevel dry air will hinder convection, and keep isolated showers \nand thunderstorms limited to the New Mexico Bootheel, Hudspeth \nCounty, and the Sacramento Mountains this afternoon. There is \nstill enough moisture for clouds to form over most of the \nlowlands, but most of these clouds will not be large enough to \nproduce precipitation and only partially obscure the sky this\nafternoon and evening. Temperatures in the lowlands will be in the\nmid-to-upper 90s.\n\nFor Thursday, moisture will increase from the southwest, and with\nit a better chance of storm coverage. The moisture plume coming \nfrom the Gulf of California will be most prominent out west in \nthe Gila National Forest and southwestern New Mexico along the \nContinental Divide, giving those regions the best chance (30-50%) \nof scattered thunderstorms on Thursday, with the Sacramento \nMountains coming in second (25-45% chance) Thursday afternoon. The\nlowlands could see a chance of isolated thunderstorms (20-40%) in\nthe overnight hours Thursday into Friday.\n\nPWAT values will peak on Friday, ranging from 1.35 to 1.45\" for\nthe lowlands, and storm coverage will be more widespread. Heavy \nrainfall from strong thunderstorms will be the main threat. \nLocalized flash flooding will be possible in flood- prone areas. \nGusty winds up to 50 mph are possible with some thunderstorms. For\nSaturday, the upper- level high will start to reform itself over \nsouthern New Mexico, and increase temperatures aloft. This will \nreduce instability and limit thunderstorm coverage. With PWAT \nvalues forecast to be around 1.3\" on Saturday, the mountains could\nstill see scattered thunderstorms and the lowlands isolated. The \nincrease in moisture should also lower temperatures slightly \ncloser to normal for the weekend.\n\nFor early next week, the upper-level high remains stationary over\nus. PWAT values are forecast to decrease to about 1.2-1.25\" \nthanks to drying in the mid-levels of the troposphere. Greater \ncoverage of thunderstorms is favored west of the Continental \nDivide, and storms are forecast be hit-or-miss for the rest of the\nregion, more so miss than hit.",
